 @EnyoTaiz  Have you even read the response? Or seen other videos? Their data is not valid. Others doing the testing have seen nowhere near the same differences. The hub's biggest issue here is they compared review data made on a content reviewer driver (something you or me never had access to even if you bought a 9070 XT the second it was up for grabs) which they compared to scores obtained on a newer updated windows 11 build + newer game builds which could have affected the outcome.

And what makes it worse is the hub in the video makes it sound as if this is attributed to driver version exclusively and more so "FineWine" which is a term that was born during the GCN days when these cards saw a performance increase due to graphics APIs evolving. That evolution was attributed to the use of async compute which boosted these cards performance. It has never once re-appeared since yet this "fine wine" stuff stuck because for one, it brings views to channels like the hub, second, it satisfies the underdog's rabid fanbase that is desperate to see AMD winning when Nvidia has been winning for over a decade now. And there's also a term that describes this phenomenon better which is "cope". Because micro details are being ignored for the sake of winning invisible arguments.

If the hub had any reviewer integrity here, this video would be delisted or the title and thumbnail rewritten to be more clarified. They would remake this video comparing their review scores with scores obtained on a fresh windows 11 build with all updates done and with the games up to date as well, the only difference being gpu driver. So it would be review date scores vs all up to date build with release day drivers (the one everyone got to use on day 1, not the reviewer driver) and the very latest drivers. I promise you the differences would paint a completely different picture that has no "FineWine" anywhere. It's insane that people still don't grasp this. If Nvidia released a crap content reviewer driver and then a proper day 1 driver, would this mean Nvidia also had the supposed "FineWine" tech? According to this video, absolutely. Truth is, it wouldn't because again, you and I never had access to the crap driver but instead had access to day the day 1 driver which fixed most of the issues. Hope this clarifies.

